---
layout: article
title: Alerts and notifications
description: Review Appwrite monitor alerts, notify organization members, and use escalations to reach the right people when an alert keeps firing.
---

An alert records one period during which a monitor's condition is met or an endpoint check is failing. Appwrite opens the alert as **Firing** and marks it **Resolved** when the monitor recovers. If the monitor fires again after it recovers, Appwrite opens a new alert.

# Review and acknowledge alerts {% #review-and-acknowledge-alerts %}

1. Open **Monitors > Alerts** in your project.
2. Filter the history or select an alert to see when it started and the usage reading or check result that caused it. For a resolved alert, you can also see the recovery time.
3. For a firing alert, click **Acknowledge** to show your team that someone is handling it. The alert keeps firing until the monitor recovers. Acknowledging also stops pending escalation steps.

# Choose who gets notified {% #choose-who-gets-notified %}

When you create or edit a monitor, use **Notify** to choose organization roles or an escalation. Confirmed members in selected roles receive email and Console notifications. Appwrite can also send push notifications to members with a registered device. Choose **Nobody, only its events** to keep the alert in the Console and use its project events without notifying members directly.

Each monitor has a severity of **Info**, **Warning**, **Error**, or **Critical**. Critical push notifications can use higher urgency on supported devices. **Cooldown** sets the minimum time between firing notifications from the same monitor. Alerts still appear in the history when notifications are subject to cooldown.

# Escalate an alert {% #escalate-an-alert %}

1. In your organization, open **Settings > Escalations** and create an escalation.
2. Add steps with the members or roles to notify and how long to wait before each step.
3. Edit the monitor, switch its recipients to an **Escalation**, and select the one you created.

Appwrite follows the steps while the alert is firing. The escalation stops when someone acknowledges the alert or the monitor recovers. If the alert keeps firing and you undo the acknowledgement, the escalation can continue from its next step.

# Use project events {% #use-project-events %}

For project monitors, use alert events to integrate with [webhooks](/docs/apis/webhooks), [Functions](/docs/products/functions), or [Realtime](/docs/apis/realtime). The event includes the alert as its payload.

| Event | When it fires |
| --- | --- |
| `monitors.[monitorId].alerts.[alertId].firing` | The monitor opens an alert. |
| `monitors.[monitorId].alerts.[alertId].resolved` | The monitor recovers and resolves the alert. |

The cooldown applies to team notifications. Project events still fire on each transition.
